# Taxonomy of self-reference

The **taxonomy of self-reference** is a classification, proposed in the
discussion behind this vault and not an established scheme, that sorts the
known examples of self-reference into eight classes by mechanism. It replaces
the attempt to force one universal definition of a [[strange-loop]] with a map
of the cases, and it says of each case which mechanism is at work.

## Description

### Why a taxonomy and not a definition

The taxonomy starts from a reading of what Hofstadter did. He had a concept,
the strange loop, that he expected to say something about intelligence, and he
defined it by example, case by case: Gödel, Escher, Bach. He did not arrive at
a formal definition. The thesis put forward in the discussion is to accept
that and work the same way, but more carefully: collect all the examples, treat
mathematical self-reference as one class among several, and classify.

The same question carried a doubt about whether a complete definition is
possible at all. A concept that describes systems describing themselves may
resist its own description, in the way the Tao that can be spoken of is not
the enduring Tao. The discussion keeps that as a [[daoist-analogy]], a remark
about descriptions and their limits, and not as a formal result.

### The eight classes

| Class | Mechanism | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| A. Recursive generation | A rule repeats on subproblems | Factorial, Fibonacci, fractals |
| B. Self-reproduction | A representation reproduces its source | Quines, self-reproducing automata |
| C. Formal diagonalization | A statement refers to its own code | Gödel sentence, Kleene fixed points |
| D. Internally definable universes | An internal construction rebuilds the universe | The constructible universe |
| E. Semantic paradoxes | Truth or meaning claims directed at themselves | Liar, Russell, Berry |
| F. Reflective computation | Programs treat programs as data | Metacircular interpreters, self-hosting compilers |
| G. Reflective adaptive systems | Models and rules are revised using results | Scientists, reflective agents |
| H. Self-modeling cognition | A reasoner represents itself in its own model | Human self-models, hypothetical AI architectures |

### How to read it

Two cautions come with the table.

- **The classes overlap.** A Gödel sentence is in class C and is also a fixed
  point. A reflective agent in class G will usually run on reflective
  computation from class F.
- **The classes are not all strange loops.** Class A is the standing
  anti-example: a factorial refers to a smaller instance of itself and crosses
  no level. Class B reproduces a description without saying anything about
  it. Only where a representation of the system takes part in the system, as
  in C and possibly in F to H, does the question of a strange loop arise.

The order of the classes is roughly an order of what is added. A repeats. B
copies. C refers. D reconstructs. E refers and breaks. F interprets. G
revises. H models the one doing the modelling.

The taxonomy was later widened beyond self-reference into a catalogue of the
transformations found in mathematics and reasoning, arranged in three layers:
operations, structural relationships and reflective phenomena. Self-reference
occupies the third layer. The operations of the first layer are collected
under [[cognitive-operation]].

## Relations

- It is offered in place of a single definition of the [[strange-loop]] {relation: contrasts}, which it treats as a concept defined by example.
- It classifies kinds of [[self-reference]] {relation: generalizes}, by mechanism.
- Class A is [[recursion]] {relation: part-of}, the anti-example.
- Class B is represented by the [[quine]] {relation: part-of}.
- Class C is [[diagonalization]] {relation: part-of}, with the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: example-of} as its instance.
- Class D is the [[constructible-universe]] {relation: part-of}.
- Class F is represented by the [[metacircular-interpreter]] {relation: part-of}.
- Classes G and H are the subject of [[reflective-agent]] {relation: part-of}.
- It sits beside the sharper variants listed in [[definitions-of-strange-loop]] {relation: analogous-to}: one sorts examples, the other sorts definitions.
- It bears on the question [[strange-loop-meaning]] {relation: answers}.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[strange-loop]].** A hierarchy of levels that is crossed and returned
  through. In this taxonomy it is not a class of its own but a property some
  members of some classes have.
- **[[quine]].** A program that prints its own source. It is the plainest
  member of class B, and shows that reproducing a description is less than
  reasoning about it.
- **[[definitions-of-strange-loop]].** Seven candidate definitions, from
  Hofstadter's to an epistemically bounded one. They line up loosely with the
  classes: Gödelian with C, generative with D, evolving reflective with G.
- **[[reflective-agent]].** A system that uses a model of itself to revise its
  own models and rules. It is where the taxonomy stops being about mathematics.

